---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CaseStudy from '../../components/CaseStudy.astro';
import Crumbs from '../../components/Crumbs.astro';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Work', url: '/work/' },
  { name: 'Language Translation', url: '/work/language-translation/' },
];

const technologies = [
  'Python',
  'TensorFlow',
  'Neural Networks',
  'Seq2Seq',
  'Attention Mechanism',
  'NLP',
];
---
<Base
  title="Neural Machine Translation"
  description="Sequence-to-sequence model for translating sentences between languages using encoder-decoder architecture with attention mechanism."
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  articleDate="2023-06-20"
>
  <main class="profile">
    <Crumbs items={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Work', href: '/work/' }, { label: 'Neural machine translation' }]} />
    <CaseStudy
      title="Neural Machine Translation"
      category="Deep Learning · NLP"
      description="A sequence-to-sequence neural network model that translates sentences from one language to another using encoder-decoder architecture with attention mechanism."
      githubUrl="https://github.com/likarajo/language_translation"
      technologies={technologies}
    >
      <section>
        <h2>Overview</h2>
        <p>
          This project implements a neural machine translation system using sequence-to-sequence
          (seq2seq) architecture with attention mechanism. The model learns to translate sentences
          from one language to another by training on parallel text corpora.
        </p>
        <p>
          Neural machine translation has transformed how we approach language translation, moving
          from rule-based and statistical methods to end-to-end learned models that can capture
          complex linguistic patterns and context.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>The Challenge</h2>
        <p>
          Machine translation presents several fundamental challenges:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Variable Length Sequences:</strong> Input and output sentences have different
            lengths and word orders
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Long-Range Dependencies:</strong> Translation requires understanding context
            from distant parts of the sentence
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Vocabulary Coverage:</strong> Handling rare words and out-of-vocabulary terms
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Semantic Preservation:</strong> Maintaining meaning while adapting to
            different grammatical structures
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Solution Approach</h2>

        <h3>Encoder-Decoder Architecture</h3>
        <p>
          The model uses a two-part architecture:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Encoder:</strong> Processes the input sentence and creates a context vector
            representing its meaning
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Decoder:</strong> Generates the translated sentence one word at a time,
            conditioned on the context vector and previously generated words
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Attention Mechanism</h3>
        <p>
          The attention mechanism addresses the bottleneck of encoding an entire sentence into
          a single fixed-length vector:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            At each decoding step, the model can "attend" to different parts of the input sequence
          </li>
          <li>
            This allows the model to focus on relevant source words when generating each target word
          </li>
          <li>
            Attention weights provide interpretability, showing which input words influenced each
            translation
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Training Process</h3>
        <p>
          The model is trained on parallel sentence pairs:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>Tokenize and encode input and target sentences</li>
          <li>Feed source sentence through encoder</li>
          <li>Use decoder to predict target sentence word-by-word</li>
          <li>Calculate loss between predicted and actual translations</li>
          <li>Update model parameters via backpropagation</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Technical Implementation</h2>

        <h3>Model Components</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Embedding Layer:</strong> Converts words to dense vector representations
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Recurrent Layers:</strong> LSTM or GRU cells for processing sequences
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Attention Layer:</strong> Computes alignment scores between encoder and
            decoder states
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Output Layer:</strong> Softmax over target vocabulary to predict next word
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Key Features</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Bidirectional encoder for better context understanding</li>
          <li>Attention visualization to interpret translation decisions</li>
          <li>Beam search for generating higher-quality translations</li>
          <li>Teacher forcing during training for faster convergence</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Results and Insights</h2>
        <p>
          The attention mechanism proved crucial for handling long sentences and maintaining
          translation quality:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Attention weights often align with intuitive word correspondences between languages
          </li>
          <li>
            The model learns to handle different word orders automatically
          </li>
          <li>
            Performance improves significantly with larger parallel corpora
          </li>
        </ul>

        <div class="highlight-box">
          <h3>Learning from Translation Patterns</h3>
          <p>
            The attention weights reveal how the model handles linguistic differences like
            adjective placement, verb conjugation, and idiomatic expressions—providing insight
            into what the neural network learned about language structure.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Applications</h2>
        <p>
          Neural machine translation techniques extend beyond language translation:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Text summarization (long text → short summary)</li>
          <li>Question answering (question + context → answer)</li>
          <li>Code generation (natural language → code)</li>
          <li>Speech recognition (audio → text)</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Key Takeaways</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Seq2seq architecture provides a flexible framework for mapping variable-length
            sequences
          </li>
          <li>
            Attention mechanism is essential for handling long-range dependencies in translation
          </li>
          <li>
            End-to-end training allows the model to learn both alignment and translation jointly
          </li>
          <li>
            Quality of parallel training data significantly impacts translation accuracy
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <p class="page-end">
        <a href="/work/">Back to all projects</a>
        {" · "}
        <a href="https://github.com/likarajo/language_translation" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GitHub</a>
      </p>
    </CaseStudy>
  </main>
</Base>
